# examples/types/enum.zag
#
# End-to-end demonstration of `enum` (bare + backed) and `union` (payload-bearing)
# per docs/manual/13-enums.md + 14-unions.md:
#   - Bare `enum` with impl method (Direction)
#   - `union` with payload variants (single-arg + multi-arg)
#   - Exhaustive match with bare-variant patterns + `_` wildcard
#   - Qualified variant construction in expression position
#   - Impl flattened to a free function (`Direction_opposite`) by codegen
#
# v1→v2 migration note: v2 splits the surface but the v1 parser is
# PERMISSIVE — both `enum` and `union` accept all 4 variant shapes:
#   - bare:               `Variant`
#   - paren-positional:   `Variant(T1, T2, ...)`
#   - brace-named-field:  `Variant { name: T, name: T }`
#   - backed-enum value:  `enum(T) { V = expr }`
# The recommended v2 surface is the strict split (enum = bare-only,
# union = payload-bearing, backed-enum = enum-only), but v1 sources
# written under the legacy "enum-accepts-everything" form still
# parse and round-trip cleanly. Migrate at your own pace.
#
# The shape that TYPE-CHECKS MATTERS:
#   - bare payload forms (`Circle(f64)`, `Rect(f64, f64)`) → `union`
#   - brace-named-field forms (`Drag { x: f64, y: f64 }`) → `union`
#   - bare-tag-only forms (`Direction.North`) → `enum` OR `union`,
#     but `enum` is the idiomatic choice (1-byte tag, no payload
#     slot).
#
# The v2.1 extension `enum(T)` backed enums lands in `examples/types/
# enum_backed.zag` (separate file because it documents a fundamentally
# different kind of type — value-categorized enumerations rather than
# bare enumerations).
#
# Note: this file was originally written when `enum` accepted both bare
# and payload forms (v1). The v2 split forces the migration below — the
# `Shape` type moved from `enum Shape { Circle(f64), Rect(f64, f64) }`
# to `union Shape { Circle(f64), Rect(f64, f64) }` because at least one
# of its variants carries a payload. `Direction` stays `enum` because
# all four of its variants are bare.

enum Direction {
    North,
    South,
    East,
    West,
}

union Shape {
    Circle(f64),
    Rect(f64, f64),
}

impl Direction {
    pub fun opposite(d: Direction) -> Direction {
        match d {
            Direction.North => Direction.South,
            Direction.South => Direction.North,
            Direction.East => Direction.West,
            _ => Direction.North,
        }
    }
}

fun main() {
    let d: Direction = Direction.North;
    let o: Direction = Direction.opposite(d);
    print(o);

    let s: Shape = Shape.Rect(3.0, 4.0);
    print(s);

    let c: Shape = Shape.Circle(2.0);
    print(c);
}
